{
  "name": "ops",
  "_comment": "Epic P — server metrics snapshot -> triage. Snapshots are shaped like the real ServerTickReport (engine/perf/ServerTickReport.h, schema v5: per-phase stats, load_factor, interest_scale, dropped_ticks, rss_kb/rss_startup_kb, congestion watermarks) plus an AuthTracker lockout summary, so the eval exercises the signals fl-ops will actually receive. Two things are scored beyond root-cause accuracy: (a) every proposed action must come from the allowlist — an agent inventing a command outside its declared runbook is exactly what Epic P's policy engine rejects; (b) the 'healthy' case exists to catch over-eagerness, where a model recommends a disruptive action against a server that is fine.\n\n#934: extended with CONGESTION-VS-SERVER-FAULT discrimination cases. Every model in the #599 sweep misread congestion — they see a low send rate and call the SERVER overloaded, when the server is healthy and one peer's LINK is bad. The distinction is operationally opposite: a congested peer needs nothing done to the server, while an overrun server needs work shed from it, and an agent that confuses them will 'fix' a healthy server under load. The discriminator is in the snapshot: load_factor near 1 with a healthy tick budget means the server is fine whatever the per-peer send rate says. Each case also names a `runbook`, so per-runbook scoring can report which PROCEDURE a model can actually follow rather than only its aggregate accuracy.",
  "budget_s": 60.0,
  "root_causes": [
    "tick_overrun",
    "congestion",
    "memory_leak",
    "auth_abuse",
    "healthy"
  ],
  "action_allowlist": [
    "status",
    "tickstats",
    "peers",
    "reload_config",
    "kick",
    "ban",
    "admin_unlock",
    "shutdown"
  ],
  "system_prompt": "You are a server-operations triage assistant for a dedicated game server. You are given a metrics snapshot. Identify the single most likely root cause and recommend diagnostic/remediation actions.\n\nContext for the metrics:\n- The server runs a fixed 60 Hz tick; the per-tick budget is 16.6 ms.\n- load_factor is the overrun governor: 1.0 = healthy, below 1.0 = the server is shedding work because ticks overran. dropped_ticks rising alongside it means it is not keeping up.\n- congestion_min_send_hz is the lowest per-client snapshot send rate reached (60 = never throttled, 10 = at the floor); congestion_max_loss is peak packet loss.\n- rss_kb vs rss_startup_kb: sustained growth with a flat entity count suggests a leak.\n- auth_lockouts counts IPs locked out for repeated failed admin passwords.\n\nRoot cause MUST be exactly one of: tick_overrun, congestion, memory_leak, auth_abuse, healthy.\n\nActions MUST be chosen ONLY from this allowlist: status, tickstats, peers, reload_config, kick, ban, admin_unlock, shutdown.\nNever invent an action outside the allowlist. If the server is healthy, recommend no disruptive action (an empty list, or read-only actions only).\n\nReply with ONLY a JSON object:\n{\"root_cause\": \"<one of the causes>\", \"actions\": [\"<allowlisted action>\", ...], \"severity\": \"low|medium|high\"}\n\nAlso name the runbook to follow, one of: shed-load | peer-link | restart-window | lockout-review | none.\n\nshed-load: the SERVER cannot hold its tick. peer-link: the server is healthy and a PEER's connection is degraded. restart-window: resource growth needs a planned restart. lockout-review: authentication abuse. none: nothing is wrong.\n\nA low per-peer send rate does NOT by itself mean the server is overloaded. Check load_factor, tick_ms and dropped_ticks: if those are healthy, the server is fine and the problem is the peer's link.\n\nInclude \"runbook\" in your JSON object alongside root_cause and actions.",
